---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base
  title="The Partial Veto"
  description="How Wisconsin's one-of-a-kind partial veto works, how it has been narrowed, and where to see it used."
>
  <h1 class="text-2xl font-bold">Wisconsin's Partial Veto</h1>
  <p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-navy-700">
    Wisconsin's governor has a power no other American governor has in this
    form: on bills that spend money, the governor can strike out parts, such as
    words, numbers or whole sections, and sign what's left into law. The
    edited version becomes the law, without going back to the Legislature.
  </p>

  <section class="mt-6 max-w-3xl">
    <h2 class="text-xl font-bold">How it works</h2>
    <ul class="mt-2 list-disc space-y-2 pl-5 text-sm text-navy-700">
      <li>
        It applies only to <strong>appropriation bills</strong>, meaning bills that
        spend money, most importantly the state budget. Other bills must be
        signed or vetoed whole.
      </li>
      <li>
        The parts the governor strikes are gone unless the Legislature
        overrides that partial veto with a <strong>two-thirds vote of both
        chambers</strong>, the same bar as overriding a full veto.
      </li>
      <li>
        The governor files a <strong>veto message</strong> explaining each
        strike; it becomes part of the bill's official record. Bill pages on
        this site link the message wherever one exists.
      </li>
      <li>
        Because striking digits and words can <em>change meaning</em>, not
        just remove spending, governors of both parties have used it to
        create outcomes the Legislature never voted on, the source of most
        partial-veto controversy.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-6 max-w-3xl">
    <h2 class="text-xl font-bold">How it has been narrowed</h2>
    <ul class="mt-2 list-disc space-y-2 pl-5 text-sm text-navy-700">
      <li>
        <strong>1930</strong>: a constitutional amendment creates the power:
        appropriation bills "may be approved in whole or in part."
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>1990</strong>: voters ban the "Vanna White" veto: striking
        individual letters within words to spell new words.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>2008</strong>: voters ban the "Frankenstein" veto: stitching
        parts of two or more sentences into a new sentence.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Still allowed</strong>: striking whole words, digits, and
        punctuation. In the 2023 budget the governor struck digits and a dash
        from a school-funding provision written to end with the "2024–25"
        school year, leaving it to run "to 2425", four extra centuries of
        annual revenue-limit increases. The Wisconsin Supreme Court upheld
        that veto in 2025.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-6 max-w-3xl">
    <h2 class="text-xl font-bold">See it in use</h2>
    <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-navy-700">
      The <a href="/vetoes/" class="underline">veto tracker</a> lists every
      full veto, partial veto, and override attempt by session, and the
      <a href="/laws/" class="underline"> New laws</a> pages flag every act
      signed with a partial veto.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-6 max-w-3xl">
    <h2 class="text-xl font-bold">Sources</h2>
    <ul class="mt-2 list-disc space-y-1 pl-5 text-sm">
